Your code is already fully set up with `IFileSystem` from `System.IO.Abstractions`. There's **nothing more to generate** — you have the right abstraction.

Here's a quick checklist to confirm you're all set:

✅ **Interface**: `IFileSystem` (from NuGet — no custom wrapper needed)
✅ **Injection**: Constructor injection via `DataService(IFileSystem fileSystem)`
✅ **Usage**: Calling `fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path)` instead of `File.ReadAllText(path)`

Just make sure you have these in place elsewhere:

**DI registration** (in `Program.cs` or `Startup.cs`):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**Test helper** (in your test project):
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data.txt", new MockFileData("hello") }
});
var service = new DataService(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("hello", service.ReadData("/data.txt"));
```

No wrapper generation needed — `System.IO.Abstractions` is the battle-tested solution you're already using. 🎉
